1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,933
An hour of redundant editing, on one angle, before he caught it.

2
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:05,501
None of it needed to happen.

3
00:00:05,501 --> 00:00:08,692
Three lectures back, you were checking Meta's safe zones.

4
00:00:08,692 --> 00:00:11,435
Two lectures ago, it was Spark Ads authorization.

5
00:00:11,435 --> 00:00:13,003
Last lecture, a skip button.

6
00:00:13,003 --> 00:00:17,706
None of that has to live in your head at once, because none of it changes the actual

7
00:00:17,706 --> 00:00:17,986
shot.

8
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:19,778
What changes is only the export.

9
00:00:19,778 --> 00:00:24,369
Generate and film in full-screen vertical, the way Section 5.1 already argued for,

10
00:00:24,369 --> 00:00:28,903
and every placement in this section becomes a crop or a caption adjustment, not a

11
00:00:28,903 --> 00:00:29,351
reshoot.

12
00:00:29,351 --> 00:00:33,886
Why does this deserve its own lecture, rather than a slide tacked onto the end of

13
00:00:33,886 --> 00:00:34,110
5.3?

14
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:38,533
Because without it, the natural instinct is to treat each platform lecture as a

15
00:00:38,533 --> 00:00:43,124
separate production task, a Meta cut, a TikTok cut, a YouTube cut, three times the

16
00:00:43,124 --> 00:00:44,188
work for one angle.

17
00:00:44,188 --> 00:00:48,723
That instinct is wrong, and it's expensive enough in real hours that it's worth a

18
00:00:48,723 --> 00:00:50,403
lecture of its own to correct.

19
00:00:50,403 --> 00:00:52,474
Think back to Section 4.6's workshop.

20
00:00:52,474 --> 00:00:54,546
You already produced three ads there.

21
00:00:54,546 --> 00:00:59,193
This lecture asks you to look at those same three files again, not to make anything

22
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:03,784
new, and work out exactly how far each one already gets you across every placement

23
00:01:03,784 --> 00:01:04,679
in this section.

24
00:01:05,229 --> 00:01:09,762
Fill this out once per angle, and notice most of the row is repetition, not new

25
00:01:09,762 --> 00:01:10,049
work.

26
00:01:10,049 --> 00:01:12,688
Four of five placements share one master file.

27
00:01:12,688 --> 00:01:17,450
Only the in-stream row, and only when Section 5.3's intent test says it's worth it,

28
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:19,400
asks for a genuinely separate cut.

29
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:24,220
That's the actual point of building this matrix instead of improvising per platform.

30
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:28,695
It turns "where does this run" into a checklist you fill out in minutes, not a

31
00:01:28,695 --> 00:01:31,276
decision you re-litigate for every single ad.

32
00:01:31,276 --> 00:01:35,809
Notice, too, what the matrix quietly assumes: that you generated in full-screen

33
00:01:35,809 --> 00:01:37,186
vertical from the start.

34
00:01:37,186 --> 00:01:41,890
If you didn't, every row above gets harder, because you're reframing up instead of

35
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:46,078
cropping down, exactly the costly direction Section 5.1 warned you about.

36
00:01:46,078 --> 00:01:49,176
The matrix rewards a decision made at generation time.

37
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:52,504
It can't undo one made the wrong way round after the fact.

38
00:01:52,504 --> 00:01:56,864
Run this same table for every angle in a batch, not just your favourite one.

39
00:01:56,864 --> 00:02:01,684
The whole point of Section 1.4's volume argument was never spending your effort on a

40
00:02:01,684 --> 00:02:02,257
single ad.

41
00:02:02,257 --> 00:02:07,077
Applying that same discipline to export work is what keeps a twenty-angle batch from

42
00:02:07,077 --> 00:02:11,896
turning into twenty separate, one-off production decisions instead of one repeatable

43
00:02:11,896 --> 00:02:12,183
pass.

44
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:16,932
One habit worth building now, before the batch gets larger than four files.

45
00:02:16,932 --> 00:02:21,634
Name every export by angle, version and placement, consistently, from the very first

46
00:02:21,634 --> 00:02:23,258
ad you ever produce this way.

47
00:02:23,258 --> 00:02:24,378
This isn't busywork.

48
00:02:24,378 --> 00:02:29,025
Section 7.3 builds a whole naming and asset-library standard on top of exactly this

49
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:33,560
pattern, and it's far easier to start consistent than to rename a folder of forty

50
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:37,702
files six weeks from now because nobody could tell which export was which.

51
00:02:37,702 --> 00:02:42,237
Notice the convention encodes exactly the information a later decision will need:

52
00:02:42,237 --> 00:02:44,701
which angle, which version, which placement.

53
00:02:44,701 --> 00:02:48,116
Nothing about performance, nothing about the claim inside it.

54
00:02:48,116 --> 00:02:49,124
That's deliberate.

55
00:02:49,124 --> 00:02:53,435
A filename is for finding a file fast, not for remembering what made it good.

56
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:57,969
That judgement belongs in the scorecard from Section 8.5, not buried inside forty

57
00:02:57,969 --> 00:03:01,721
characters of a filename nobody will read carefully under pressure.

58
00:03:01,721 --> 00:03:06,423
Ask yourself, honestly: could you tell, right now, which of your existing exports is

59
00:03:06,423 --> 00:03:08,607
which without opening every single one?

60
00:03:08,607 --> 00:03:11,574
If the answer's no, that's not a small inconvenience.

61
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:16,053
It's the exact failure mode Section 8's results reading depends on you avoiding,

62
00:03:16,053 --> 00:03:20,700
because a scorecard is useless if you can't confidently match a winning result back

63
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,715
to the specific file that earned it.

64
00:03:23,265 --> 00:03:26,917
One honest limit on what a matrix like this can actually do for you.

65
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:28,796
It tells you which file goes where.

66
00:03:28,796 --> 00:03:33,252
It doesn't tell you whether Meta's safe zones moved, or whether your Spark Ads code

67
00:03:33,252 --> 00:03:37,494
is about to expire, or whether an audience is intent-driven enough to justify a

68
00:03:37,494 --> 00:03:38,729
separate in-stream cut.

69
00:03:38,729 --> 00:03:43,186
Those are still judgement calls, made fresh, the way the last three lectures taught

70
00:03:43,186 --> 00:03:44,099
you to make them.

71
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:46,998
The matrix removes the busywork around that judgement.

72
00:03:46,998 --> 00:03:49,683
It was never going to remove the judgement itself.

73
00:03:49,683 --> 00:03:53,925
That's a distinction worth being honest about, because it's tempting to treat a

74
00:03:53,925 --> 00:03:56,931
checklist like this as a substitute for staying current.

75
00:03:56,931 --> 00:03:57,415
It isn't.

76
00:03:57,415 --> 00:04:01,818
Revisit the platform-specific rows in this matrix on the same quarterly rhythm the

77
00:04:01,818 --> 00:04:06,328
last three lectures already asked of you, not because the matrix itself changes, but

78
00:04:06,328 --> 00:04:08,422
because what belongs in each cell does.

79
00:04:08,972 --> 00:04:13,955
Next lecture asks a question this whole section has been circling: once you hand a

80
00:04:13,955 --> 00:04:18,937
platform its export, how much of what happens next is still your decision, and how

81
00:04:18,937 --> 00:04:23,556
much of it is the platform's own automated delivery system deciding for you.

82
00:04:23,556 --> 00:04:25,743
Before that, do the actual exercise.

83
00:04:25,743 --> 00:04:30,665
Take one angle from your Section 4.6 workshop, artifact B04, fill in every row of

84
00:04:30,665 --> 00:04:34,676
this matrix for it, and name the files using the convention above.

85
00:04:34,676 --> 00:04:39,172
It's the last piece of production discipline this section asks of you, and

86
00:04:39,172 --> 00:04:44,094
everything from Section 7 onward, including B06's naming standard, assumes you've

87
00:04:44,094 --> 00:04:45,553
already got it in place.
